  "name": "Sekkyō-bushi (Hachiōji sermon-ballad narrative)",
  "family": "classical",
  "lineage": "Sekkyō-bushi, a Japanese narrative song, grew out of medieval Buddhist sermon-ballads telling tales such as Sanshō Dayū and Oguri Hangan, and in the early Edo period became a shamisen-accompanied puppet-theatre narrative (sekkyō jōruri) before being eclipsed by gidayū-bushi. Late in the Edo period Satsuma Wakatayū revived it as sekkyō saimon, which spread to Hachiōji by the Meiji era; there the Satsuma and Wakamatsu schools still narrate the kuruma ningyō cart-puppet theatre.",
  "recipe": "classic modern sekkyō-bushi, art Music, east Asian branch. narrative, solo with instrumental accompaniment. cat skin ivory min yo folk song accompaniment shamisen. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
